      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay that makes more sense thanks. so once the agent has the money and the papers, how do they actually decide when it's safe to release the funds? like what specifically triggers them to send the money to the seller?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The escrow agent follows a specific timeline and set of conditions outlined in the purchase agreement to determine when it's safe to release the funds to the seller. Here are some common triggers that may prompt the escrow agent to release the funds:\n\n1. Loan approval: Once the buyer's lender approves the loan and provides the necessary documents, the escrow agent can release the funds to the seller.\n2. Property inspection: If the buyer's home inspection reveals no major issues, the escrow agent can release the funds to the seller.\n3. Appraisal: If the property appraisal meets or exceeds the purchase price, the escrow agent can release the funds to the seller.\n4. Title clearance: If the title search reveals no issues or liens, the escrow agent can release the funds to the seller.\n5. Closing date: Once all parties have signed the closing documents and the funds have been transferred to the escrow account, the escrow agent can release the funds to the seller.\n\nThe escrow agent will typically communicate with the buyer, seller, and other parties involved to ensure that all conditions have been met before releasing the funds. Once the conditions are satisfied, the escrow agent will send the funds to the seller's attorney, who will then disburse the funds to the seller.",
